      <h2>Why Tom Yum Is the Ultimate Keto Soup</h2>
      <p>Tom Yum is arguably the most keto-friendly dish in all of Thai cuisine. It's naturally low in carbohydrates, high in flavor, and requires no special substitutions to be keto-compatible. The classic version — Tom Yum Goong (with shrimp) — contains almost zero carbs from the broth, with only the mushrooms and a small amount of vegetables contributing to the carb count.</p>

      <h2>Two Styles of Tom Yum</h2>
      <p>There are two main versions of Tom Yum, and both are keto-friendly:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tom Yum Nam Sai (clear):</strong> The classic clear broth version. Lighter, spicier, and more sour. This is the most keto-friendly option at ~3-4g net carbs per serving.</li>
        <li><strong>Tom Yum Nam Khon (creamy):</strong> The richer version with added coconut milk or evaporated milk. Creamier and slightly milder. About 5-7g net carbs per serving with coconut milk.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Keto Tom Yum Goong (Clear Version)</h2>
      <h3>Ingredients (Serves 4)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>1 lb large shrimp, shell-on (peel and devein, reserve shells)</li>
        <li>6 cups water or chicken stock</li>
        <li>3 stalks lemongrass, bruised and cut into 2-inch pieces</li>
        <li>5-6 slices galangal (about 1/4 inch thick)</li>
        <li>5-6 kaffir lime leaves, torn</li>
        <li>5-6 Thai bird chilies, crushed (adjust to heat preference)</li>
        <li>8 oz straw mushrooms (or button mushrooms), halved</li>
        <li>2-3 tbsp fish sauce</li>
        <li>3-4 tbsp fresh lime juice</li>
        <li>Fresh cilantro and Thai chili paste (nam prik pao) for serving</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Instructions</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Make shrimp stock (optional but recommended):</strong> Sauté shrimp shells in a dry pot until pink and fragrant. Add water, bring to a boil, and simmer 10 minutes. Strain and discard shells. This step adds incredible depth.</li>
        <li>Bring the stock or water to a boil. Add lemongrass, galangal, and kaffir lime leaves. Boil for 3-4 minutes until fragrant.</li>
        <li>Add mushrooms and cook for 2-3 minutes.</li>
        <li>Add shrimp and cook just until pink (about 2 minutes). Do not overcook.</li>
        <li>Remove from heat. Stir in fish sauce and lime juice. Taste and adjust — it should be sour, salty, and spicy in perfect balance.</li>
        <li>Serve immediately, garnished with fresh cilantro and a spoonful of nam prik pao (Thai chili paste) on the side.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Macros per serving:</strong> ~180 calories, 4g fat, 4g net carbs, 28g protein</p>

      <h2>Creamy Keto Tom Yum (Nam Khon)</h2>
      <p>For the creamy version, add 1 cup of full-fat coconut milk after step 4 and simmer for 2 more minutes before seasoning. This adds healthy fats and makes the soup more substantial as a meal.</p>
      <p><strong>Macros per serving (creamy):</strong> ~280 calories, 18g fat, 6g net carbs, 28g protein</p>

      <h2>Key Ingredients Explained</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lemongrass:</strong> Provides the signature citrusy aroma. Bruise it with the back of a knife to release the oils. Don't eat it — it's fibrous.</li>
        <li><strong>Galangal:</strong> Similar to ginger but more citrusy and piney. Don't substitute ginger — the flavor is different. Like lemongrass, it's for flavor, not eating.</li>
        <li><strong>Kaffir lime leaves:</strong> The most important aromatic. Tear them to release the oils. They add an unmistakable citrus-floral note.</li>
        <li><strong>Fish sauce:</strong> Provides the salty umami backbone. Don't skip it — there's no substitute.</li>
        <li><strong>Nam prik pao:</strong> Thai roasted chili paste. Adds smoky depth and a touch of sweetness. Use sparingly on keto (about 1-2g carbs per teaspoon).</li>
      </ul>
